  "text": "The traditional cause‑and‑effect picture that works well for everyday objects breaks down when we look at the tiniest particles. At that scale, each event is so intimately linked with the measuring device that we cannot talk about the particle’s behavior independently of the experiment that observes it. This intertwining forces us to accept a new kind of link between results obtained from different experimental arrangements, a relationship that shows the same system can display mutually exclusive features depending on how we look at it. The mathematical structure of the modern microscopic theory provides a suitable way to describe these patterns, allowing us to predict regularities that are either exact or statistical, which cannot be explained by the old classical cause‑and‑effect framework."
